 \begin{abstract}
-Cryptography is fundamental to protecting critical data in network information security. This paper introduces a new generalised quantum encryption framework (QEF) designed to encrypt any quantum image representation (QIR). The framework uses a three-layer unitary operation comprising substitution, permutation, and entanglement layers. In the first layer, random angles generated from the \(n\)-dimensional quantum hyperchaotic system (nD-QHCS) modify pixel colour information by targeting the colour or feature qubits and rotating their angles. In the second layer, the permutation operation rearranges pixel positions according to discrete permutation keys generated by the HG\(\Phi\)-QRNG, whose binary outcomes follow the Bernoulli probability distribution derived from the non-adiabatic, non-cyclic Pancharatnam--Samuel--Bhandari (PSB) open-path geometric phases. In the third layer, the binary outputs from the HG\(\Phi\)-QRNG control entanglement gates between selected colour and position qubits, spreading quantum correlations throughout the image state. This process supports integrity, strengthens resistance to tampering, and transforms the encryption into a hybrid quantum-secure scheme. A comprehensive quantitative evaluation was conducted to assess the effectiveness of the proposed QEF and the fidelity of decrypted images using standard image-quality, similarity, and error-analysis measures. Security against quantum attacks, including the Grover search attack (GSA), quantum chosen-plaintext attack (QCPA), quantum known-plaintext attack (QKPA), and quantum state tomography attack, is demonstrated. Computational measures for the QEF are also introduced, including a general formula for quantum circuit depth ($\mathcal{QCD}$) for all QIR models, the number of quantum gate compositions ($\mathcal{QGC}$), and the quantum gate cost $QC_{\mathrm{QEF}}$. In addition, the Lean theorem prover was used to provide machine-checked formal validation of the mathematical derivations and reduce potential vulnerabilities. The proposed framework offers scalability, flexibility across QIR models, and resistance to quantum and classical attacks, establishing a foundation for future secure quantum communication protocols involving quantum images.
-
-\vspace{0.5cm}
-\noindent \textbf{Keywords:} quantum cryptography, dynamic modulation, geometric phase, hyperchaotic systems, Pancharatnam--Samuel--Bhandari geometric phase, Lean 4 formalisation.
+{\color{red}
+This paper presents a quantum encryption framework (QEF) for image
+representations admitting a position--feature decomposition and unitary
+state preparation. Its three unitary layers are pixel-controlled Euler
+rotations, reversible position permutation, and position-controlled
+CNOT/CZ operations. The nD-QHCS supplies substitution parameters, while
+the HG$\Phi$-QRNG supplies permutation and entanglement-control material.
+Unitarity establishes exact ideal decryption. We distinguish this
+correctness result from confidentiality and derive a conditional
+sample-complexity lower bound for distinguishing two images encrypted
+with independently refreshed keys. The bound follows from the actual
+rotation moments and quantum information inequalities; it does not
+assume arbitrary-unitary reconstruction or exhaustive key search.
+Explicit finite-angle and continuous reference laws are analyzed, and
+the additional source conditions needed to apply the bound to the
+original generators are identified. Image-quality simulations provide
+scrambling and recovery diagnostics, rather than a certification of
+those source conditions. Circuit-resource expressions and Lean~4
+unitarity checks complement the analysis. The framework provides a
+common reversible architecture, while numerical cryptographic
+certification of its original key sources remains an open requirement.
+\par}
+
 \end{abstract}
 
 
