 Based on the weaknesses and limitations identified in existing quantum image encryption models, this work introduces a QEF based on the hybrid nD-QHCS/HG\(\Phi\)-QRNG generated via non-adiabatic, non-cyclic Pancharatnam--Samuel--Bhandari (PSB) open-path geometric phases \cite{Alwan2026}. The QEF is designed with three fundamental layers: substitution, permutation, and entanglement. Each layer performs a specific cryptographic task. The substitution layer achieves confusion, while the permutation layer ensures diffusion, following Shannon's classical security principles. In addition, a dedicated entanglement operation is incorporated to increase complexity and strengthen the correlation between colour and position qubits within the quantum image.
 The proposed QEF was applied to several QIR models and produced promising results compared with other quantum image encryption techniques. A set of important performance metrics was also collected, including quantum circuit depth (QCD), the number of computational quantum gates, and quantum cost. First, a general mathematical formula was derived for these metrics; then, exact values were computed for each QIR model after applying the proposed QEF.
 
