-
-\paragraph{Grover Search Attack GSA:}
-It provides a quadratic speedup for exhaustive key search. 
-Therefore, if the size of the secret key space is \(N\), a classical brute-force attack needs \(O(N)\) trials, whereas GSA reduces the required complexity to \(O(\sqrt{N})\).
-
-In the QEF scheme, the total secret key $K_{\rm total}$ is constructed from three independent components: 
-the substitution key \(K_i^{\rm sub}\), the permutation key \(K^{\rm perm}\), and the entanglement key \(K^{\rm ent}\). Hence, the $K_{\rm total}$ can be expressed as
-\[
-K_{\rm total}
-=
-K_i^{\rm sub}
-\parallel
-K^{\rm perm}
-\parallel
-K^{\rm ent}.
-\]
-
-The corresponding lengths of $K_{\rm total}$ are \(L_{\rm sub}\), \(L_{\rm perm}\), and \(L_{\rm ent}\), respectively. 
-Then, the total key length $L_{\rm total}$ is defined as:
-\[
-L_{\rm total}
-=
-L_{\rm sub}
-+
-L_{\rm perm}
-+
-L_{\rm ent}.
-\]
-
-Accordingly, the $K_{\rm total}$ is given by
-\[
-|\mathcal{K}_{\rm total}|
-=
-|\mathcal{K}_{\rm sub}|
-\cdot
-|\mathcal{K}_{\rm perm}|
-\cdot
-|\mathcal{K}_{\rm ent}|
-=
-2^{L_{\rm total}}.
-\]
-
-The QEF encryption operation can be modeled as a composition of three key-dependent quantum unitary transformations:
-\[
-|\psi_{\rm cipher}\rangle
-=
-U_E(K^{\rm ent})
-P_{\pi_y}(K^{\rm perm})
-R_{\rm sub}(K_i^{\rm sub})
-|\psi_{\rm plain}\rangle,
-\]
-where \(R_{\rm sub}\), \(P_{\pi_y}\) and  \(U_E\) are the substitution,  permutation, and entanglement-based masking operations, respectively.
-
-To recover the O state correctly, an adversary must recover the complete key tuple $
-\left(
-K_i^{\rm sub},
-K^{\rm perm},
-K^{\rm ent}
-\right).
-$
-Any incorrect component leads to an invalid decryption result; such as;
-\[
-D_{\tilde{K}_{\rm total}}(|\psi_{\rm cipher}\rangle)
-\neq
-|\psi_{\rm plain}\rangle,
-\qquad
-\tilde{K}_{\rm total}
-\neq
-K_{\rm total}.
-\]
-
-Therefore, the classical exhaustive-search complexity is
-\[
-T_{\rm classical}
-=
-O\left(2^{L_{\rm total}}\right).
-\]
-
-Under GSA, this complexity is reduced to
-\[
-T_{\rm Grover}
-=
-O\left(\sqrt{2^{L_{\rm total}}}\right)
-=
-O\left(2^{L_{\rm total}/2}\right).
-\]
-
-To maintain at least \(128\)-bit security against GSA, the following 
